    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Define the outcome for an Account Handoff That Preserves Context",
        "body": "An Account Handoff That Preserves Context begins with a result the account team can inspect without asking someone to reconstruct the week from memory. Write the finish line as a visible state in the account review queue: what was checked, which source governed the check, who owns the next action, and what would reopen the item. For customers, that clarity matters because a tidy status can still hide missing evidence.\n\nSeparate the preparation work from the decision that follows it. A trained assistant can collect, compare, label, and route information in the CRM. The accountable owner still decides eligibility, approval, clinical or financial effect, and any exception that changes the customer's position. Keep that boundary in the written brief, not only in a manager's head."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Map the inputs and the record",
        "body": "Start with the account record and the source systems that feed it. Name the exact fields the assistant may read, the approved places to look, and the point where a source is missing, stale, or contradictory. A useful rule is to record the source, version, and timestamp beside every material fact rather than copying an unexplained value into the account review queue.\n\nInclude the handoff details that are normally learned by asking around: request templates, naming rules, duplicate checks, required attachments, and the owner who answers ambiguous questions. Store the brief next to the work so it can be corrected when policy or system behavior changes. A document that lives three folders away will not survive a busy account team week."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Build the minimum evidence set",
        "body": "For an Account Handoff That Preserves Context, the minimum evidence set should let a reviewer answer four questions: what was requested, what arrived, what was verified, and what remains unresolved. Keep one durable link per item and note the date or version that makes the evidence current. Avoid screenshots pasted into free text when a stable system reference exists.\n\nUse structured statuses rather than vague notes such as \"working on it.\" A short set of states, for example received, incomplete, ready for review, exception, and closed, makes the account review queue filterable and gives the assistant a safe place to stop. Every exception should carry a reason code and the name of the role that must resolve it."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Write the exception path first",
        "body": "Most failures in an Account Handoff That Preserves Context come from ordinary exceptions that were never designed: a customer sends an expired document, two systems disagree, a deadline moves, or a request arrives outside the approved channel. Decide in advance which of these the assistant may resolve with a documented rule and which must return to the account team immediately.\n\nWrite the escalation as a short chain: what was observed, what the assistant already checked, what decision is needed, and by when. This turns an interruption into a reviewable item and prevents the assistant from guessing. The chain should also say what the assistant must not do, including promising an outcome, changing a protected field, or contacting a party outside the approved script."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Set access and review boundaries",
        "body": "Grant access to the CRM and account record only at the level the workflow requires. Use named accounts, least-privilege roles, and a documented removal step when the assignment ends. If the assistant needs to send customer communication, provide approved language and a clear list of questions that must be routed rather than answered.\n\nReview a small sample at a predictable interval. Look for missing source notes, unclear ownership, repeated exceptions, and statuses that moved without evidence. Correct the checklist or the source guidance when a pattern appears. The purpose of review is to improve the operating system, not to inspect every keystroke."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Measure whether the account review queue is healthier",
        "body": "Track a few signals that reflect the work rather than raw activity: items with complete evidence, items waiting on the wrong owner, exceptions by reason, reopened records, and the time between receipt and a clear next step. These measures show whether an Account Handoff That Preserves Context is becoming easier to audit and hand off.\n\nDo not treat message volume or task count as proof of quality. A large log can hide weak records. Review whether a second person can understand the state of the account record in a few minutes, whether the customer received accurate information, and whether the accountable owner had to redo the preparation."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Pilot the workflow before scaling it",
        "body": "Run one controlled cohort of an Account Handoff That Preserves Context with a small, representative sample. Include an ordinary case, a missing-evidence case, a conflicting-source case, and a late update. Compare the prepared output with the reviewer's expectation and record the differences. Use the pilot to narrow the task, not to prove that every case can be delegated.\n\nOnly expand after the checklist, access, and escalation path hold under normal volume. Then add a second workflow or a second account team queue. A precise role with a reliable handoff is easier to manage and more useful than a broad request for help that changes shape every week."
      }
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Name the accountable owner.",
      "Preserve the source record.",
      "Route exceptions before closing."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "What should stay with the account team?",
        "a": "Approval, policy interpretation, regulated judgments, sensitive exceptions, and any decision that changes the customer's rights or position should remain with the accountable owner."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is the safest first task to delegate?",
        "a": "Choose a repeating task with a stable source, a visible output in the account review queue, and a named reviewer who can answer exceptions."
      },
      {
        "q": "How should quality be reviewed?",
        "a": "Sample the output against a short evidence checklist, then fix the process when the same gap appears more than once."
      }
    ]
